<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khujand</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Leninabad, Khodjent</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khujand is the second city of Tajikistan, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> at the entrance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Fergana Valley</a>. One of the oldest towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, it is identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria-eschate">Alexandria Eschate</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> in 329 BCE, and was a station on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>. Known as Leninabad from 1936 to 1991, its region supplied much of the Soviet-era Tajik ruling elite.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khujand.jpg" alt="Khujand" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: EH · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_to_Khujand.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1137 he defeated the Western Karakhanids near <b>Khujand</b> and annexed Fergana and Tashkent, his army swollen by 10,000 Khitans formerly subject to the Karakhanids, and went on to take Kashgar, Khotan and Beshbalik.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1929 the republic was separated from Uzbekistan and raised to a union republic, the Tajik SSR, with the <b>Khujand</b> region added.</blockquote><a href="/state/tajik-assr">Tajik ASSR</a></li><li><blockquote>In October 1929 the Tajik ASSR was separated from the Uzbek SSR and upgraded to a union republic, the Tajik SSR, with the addition of the <b>Khujand</b> district.</blockquote><a href="/state/tajik-ssr">Tajik SSR</a></li><li><blockquote>Tension between the old communist elite, largely from <b>Khujand</b> and Kulob, and an opposition of democrats, Islamists and Pamiris from Gharm and Gorno-Badakhshan erupted into civil war in 1992.</blockquote><a href="/state/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a></li></ul></section>
